   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the tax law, in relation to authorizing the city of White Plains to impose an occupancy tax",
    "basePrintNo": "S3303",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S3303-2015",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §1202-aa, Tax L",
    "lawSection": "Tax Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S3303\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the tax law, in relation to authorizing the city of\nWhite Plains to impose an occupancy tax\n\nPURPOSE:\n\nTo authorize the City of White Plains to extend a room occupancy tax\nnot to exceed 3%, for the three year period expiring December 31,\n2018.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1202 of the tax law is amended to extend authority of the City\nof White Plains to enact such a tax, for a three year period expiring\nDecember 31, 2018.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThe city of White Plains seeks to continue the authority granted in\n2009 to impose a 3% daily room occupancy tax upon any hotel, motel, or\nsimilar place in the City. The occupancy tax is a non-resident tax\nwhich has lessened the burden of the real property tax on residents\nand has allowed the city to remain within the State 2% property tax\ncap.\n\nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nA.3123/S.1086 - Signed into law as Chapter 95 of the Laws of 2009\nA.10513/S.7510 - Signed into law as Chapter 355 of the Laws of 2012\n\nFISCAL STATE AND LOCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nThe City of White Plains received $969,632 in revenues from this tax\nin the fiscal year ending June 30, 2014 and anticipates approximately\n$1 million in revenues for each of the three years covered by this\nbill request. There is no fiscal impact to New York State.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n\nImmediately.\n\n",
